Chapter: EffectWidenArg
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A function that performs less than it was asked for is safe to hand over.
 writer performs Console.Write; apply-it asks for anything Console. The def
 boundary has always allowed this direction -- a [Console] def may perform
 Console.Write -- but the argument boundary compared rows for equality and
 turned this program away with CDX2090. That was BACKLOG 1.3, and it turned
 away a program that could not go wrong: apply-it already promised its own
 caller it might do something Console-ish, and writing is something
 Console-ish.

 The counterpart, that the unsafe direction is still refused, is pinned by
 errors/effect-widen-arg-deny. Both are needed: a check that only ever says
 yes is not a check.

Section: Probe

  writer : Text -> [Console.Write] Nothing
  writer (t) = print-line-uni t

  apply-it : (Text -> [Console] Nothing), Text -> [Console] Nothing
  apply-it (f) (t) = f t

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    apply-it writer "widened"
  end
